From 16b5f17b2699ef860d0212fbca04e84e07d3767f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dominggoes Isakh <drjisakh@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:44:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] lmkd: ignore LMK_START_MONITORING with the in-kernel LMK

ActivityManager sends LMK_START_MONITORING after boot. In in-kernel mode
lmkd never registers PSI monitors, so monitors_initialized is false and
it calls init_monitors(). Kernels with the in-kernel low memory killer
usually have no PSI, so that fails and lmkd exits with status 1, to be
restarted by init, on every boot.

The kernel driver does the monitoring itself, so there is nothing to
start.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: Ibf24bcbb3b9544a6f9bd2375a9086cad825c0242
---
 lmkd.cpp | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/lmkd.cpp b/lmkd.cpp
index 4bac215..7a298d1 100644
--- a/lmkd.cpp
+++ b/lmkd.cpp
@@ -1627,6 +1627,10 @@ static void ctrl_command_handler(int dsock_idx) {
         // Registration is needed only if it was skipped earlier.
         if (monitors_initialized)
             return;
+        // The in-kernel low memory killer does the monitoring itself, and
+        // kernels that have it usually lack PSI, so init_monitors() would fail.
+        if (use_inkernel_interface)
+            return;
         if (!property_get_bool("sys.boot_completed", false)) {
             ALOGE("LMK_START_MONITORING cannot be handled before boot completed");
             return;
